-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  dir_message.pcpp - Directive compliance: @message

  @message <severity> "<text>"; reports <text> at compile time, at the
  severity given: hint, warn, error or fatal (BNF.md sec 2). It is allowed
  in every module (an imported unit too) and it is also a statement, wherever
  a statement stands, match arms included (sec 5). A body @message reports
  once, when the body is analysed, whether or not the code ever runs. An
  @message in an inactive @ifdef branch reports nothing. hint and warn let
  the build go on; error and fatal fail it (the break files below).

  This program reports a hint and a warning from its prologue, a hint from a
  routine that is never called, a warning from a match arm, a hint from the
  main block, and its unit dir_message_unit.pcpp a hint from its prologue
  and a warning from a routine body: seven reports, each on the line its
  // HINT / WARN comment marks. Nothing at run time changes: the program
  prints what its statements print.

  How to check, from repo\bin:
    pcpp res\tests\dir_message.pcpp -r
    pcpp res\tests\dir_message.pcpp -r -t x86_64-linux-gnu   (runs in WSL)
  The build shows the seven reports (no "never reported" text), then the
  program prints:
  EXPECT:
    before
    arm 1
    dir_message_unit.Value() = 7
    [Heap] Allocs: 0, Frees: 0, Leaked: 0
  EXITCODE: 0
  Both targets print the same; the [Heap] line is the debug build's heap report.

  Break it: run each file below the same way, without -r; each reports
  exactly what the // ERROR / FATAL comment on its marked line says, and
  nothing else:
    dir_message_err_error.pcpp      @message error: the build fails
    dir_message_err_fatal.pcpp      @message fatal, as a statement: the build fails
    dir_message_err_severity.pcpp   a severity that is not one of the four
    dir_message_err_text.pcpp       no text
    dir_message_err_string.pcpp     a text that is not a plain string
*******************************************************************************/

module exe dir_message;
@message hint "dir_message: a hint from the prologue"; // HINT SM0108@1: a hint from the prologue
@message warn "dir_message: a warning from the prologue"; // WARN SM0108@1: a warning from the prologue
@ifdef DIR_MESSAGE_NEVER
@message error "dir_message: never reported (inactive branch)";
@endif
import dir_message_unit;

routine Unused();
begin
  @message hint "dir_message: in a routine never called"; // HINT SM0108@3: in a routine never called
end;

routine Show(AN: int32);
begin
  match AN of
    1:
      @message warn "dir_message: in a match arm"; // WARN SM0108@7: in a match arm
      println("arm 1");
  else
    println("other");
  end;
end;

begin
  println("before");
  @message hint "dir_message: in the main block"; // HINT SM0108@3: in the main block
  Show(1);
  println("dir_message_unit.Value() = {}", dir_message_unit.Value());
end.
